  "notes": "Mid-tempo Texas-adjacent honky-tonk, and the entry's defining feature is the shape of the tempo distribution rather than any one number: \"BPM 50–161 … with an unusually dense mid cluster 92–132 that is the entry's defining feature\", from sixteen reachable readings — \"the best-documented tempo distribution in the section\". bpm.mode 112 sits in the middle of that cluster. The single most specific production fact in the entry is the fill rule: he was produced across the hit run by James Stroud, \"a career session drummer — which shows in the records as groove-first arrangements with fills only at 8-bar boundaries\", so drums.fills is authored 8/8 rather than the genre's 4/8, and that is this model's own number. The rest of the kit is the entry's: a shuffled two-beat with the lope on a closed hat and a dry snare in the mid cluster, a quarter kick and a narrower ratio on the 145–161 cuts (kick densityPerBar 2–4), and cross-stick on the ballads with the kit entering at the chorus (the rim lane at −6 dB). Harmony is the entry's list — I–IV–V with V7, ii–V turnarounds and I–V–vi–IV on the ballads. The vocal is the documented \"warm mid-baritone with a nasal edge, conversational verse phrasing in a narrow fifth, chorus a fourth up on sustained notes; ornament limited to a short scoop and a slow terminal vibrato\", so the melody is stepwise-heavy at 0.7 with endVariation on and a narrow 52–76 register. The counter is the stated pedal steel and fiddle \"trading 2-bar answer phrases\", so answer_lick dominates at a 2-bar phrase length. ⚠ INFERRED: swing.amount 0.66, all velocity tiers, ghost probability and vel, hat and percs densities. The per-track BPM readings are bpmdatabase values and [LOW-CONF] as a class, and the research marks the harmony as generalised [LOW-CONF]; confidence is medium because the research rates the entry medium-high.",
